## Done versus planned

The news hook is that LG hosted Nvidia officials at the Data Factory shortly after the MOU. Reading the two pages together, the confirmed items are fairly modest: the August MOU, the July business centre, the Data Factory under construction, CLOiD robots generating data, and, per LG, the use of Omniverse, Cosmos and Isaac. Everything else is future tense.

LG's stated plans are all for the end of 2026. It says the Data Factory is scheduled to be fully operational by then and will house several hundred robots. It expects training data, real plus Cosmos-generated, to total 100,000 hours, which LG equates to roughly 12 years of data. LG says the data will be used to advance its Robot Foundation Model, which LG says underpins the performance of humanoid robots, and to expand from industrial and commercial robots into home robots.

These are expectations, not results. As of the date these pages were read, 22 November 2026, this post has not found a statement on either page that the targets have been met, and it did not search for later updates.

## What neither page says

Some of the most-repeated claims about this partnership do not appear on the official pages. Neither page gives a launch date for a humanoid or home robot, a price, a list of supported tasks, or a country-by-country availability plan. Neither article body mentions a trade-show debut. If a reader comes across a report of a specific launch date, it should be treated as a media claim until LG or Nvidia states it on a page of their own; this post has not checked any such report.

The pages also give no performance figures. There is no information on how well CLOiD works, how many tasks it can do, or how much of the 100,000-hour target consists of real versus synthetic data. LG's description of its robots as "deployed at scale" is the company's own phrasing and the pages give no number of units.

## Why it matters for everyday buyers

For small businesses and households, the practical takeaway is that this is infrastructure news. LG is investing in the data and simulation side of robotics before any consumer product has a published date. If a home robot from LG does arrive, the useful questions will be the ones these pages leave open: what it can do, what it costs, what data it collects in a home, and how long it is supported. Those answers will have to come from LG's product pages, not from a data-collection announcement. This is general information about a corporate announcement, not purchasing, financial or investment advice.

## What to watch next

A few concrete things would turn plans into facts. LG saying the Data Factory is fully operational, a reported count of robots and hours of data, a named humanoid or home robot product with specifications, and an Nvidia statement on the reference robots it says it plans to build with LG. Until then, the honest summary is a signed cooperation agreement, a facility under construction, and a set of year-end goals.
